<rules>
INTRO (2-3 sentences, Ukrainian, plain text):
  - Start with a warm evening greeting ("Добрий вечір! 🌇" or similar)
  - Name the current day of week ({{ weekday_uk }})
  - In 1 sentence: name 2-3 key themes of today ("від ... до ...")
  - Natural, not salesy

ITEMS: Exactly 10 news articles from the provided list. For each:
  - emoji: one topic-fitting emoji (⚖️ for legal, ⛽ for fuel, 🏫 for schools, ☀️ for weather, etc.)
  - title: short sharp headline (5-10 Ukrainian words), rewritten shorter than original
  - hook: one-line teaser or question that intrigues the reader to click (3-8 words). Examples: "Португалії загрожує штраф", "Ресторани на межі вимирання", "Що буде далі?"
  - slug: exact slug from the provided list
  Order by importance/impact, most important first.
  If fewer than 10 news are provided, include all of them and repeat articles in importance order — but prefer to keep only news with type=news.

GLOSSARY: Exactly 2 Portuguese words that appear in today's articles, with Ukrainian translations.
  - Choose words that are practically useful for a Ukrainian immigrant (bureaucracy, everyday life, news vocabulary)
  - Format: short (1-2 words PT) — short (1-3 words UA)
  - Examples from user's preferred style:
    * acolhimento — прийняття
    * gratuito — безкоштовний
    * equivalência — визнання
    * imposto — податок

IMAGE_PROMPT (English, 140-220 words, for gpt-image-1 high quality):
  Compose a single PASHTELKA NEWS DIGEST COVER — a hand-drawn comic-book panorama in Pashtelka's house style (clean bold outlines, warm pastel palette: cream, peach, soft gold, terracotta, sky blue). NOT photorealistic. NO real photos, NO real faces, NO real branded logos.

  HERO (mandatory, large, instantly readable):
    A stylised PASTEL DE NATA — the custard tart with caramelised top and a cheerful smiling face on the custard surface. Roughly 25-35% of canvas height, clearly the focal point. Pose / placement should suit the day's mood (e.g. perched on a tram window, sitting at a cafe table reading a newspaper, peeking out of a kiosk).

  SETTING: a Lisbon or Porto street vignette — azulejo tiles, pastel buildings, tram lines, awnings — chosen to fit the dominant story themes. Day or warm golden-hour light.

  IN-WORLD SIGNAGE (key — replaces a collage):
    Around the hero, naturally integrate 4-6 storefront signs, kiosk newspapers, café chalkboards, tram destination boards, painted shop windows, posters, or graffiti tags. Each one carries a SHORT VISUAL CUE (icon + 1-3 word label) that hints at one of today's news items — e.g. a fuel-pump icon with "PALNE", a court gavel with "TRIBUNAL", a metro logo with "GREVE", a sun + waves icon, a stork over an empty hospital, etc. Cues must be language-agnostic / international — small Latin letters or pure pictograms only, never long Cyrillic phrases. Pick cues from the 10 articles, prioritising the 3-4 most impactful.

  COMPOSITION: dense Where's-Wally readability — the eye finds the hero first, then 4-6 signage cues, then ambient detail. Single coherent illustration, not a grid or collage.

  EXPLICIT NEGATIVES: no real photographs, no human faces, no recognisable brand logos, no long text blocks, no watermarks.
</rules>
